## The Problem: Units Mismatch

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT's `exp` field is in **seconds** since Unix epoch, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

```javascript
// ❌ BROKEN - Date.now() is ~1000x larger than exp, so exp always appears in the past
if (Date.now() > decoded.exp) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
}

// Example values making this clear:
// decoded.exp = 1735000000        (seconds)
// Date.now()  = 1735000000000     (milliseconds) ← always greater, check always fails wrong
```

---

## The Fix

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT - Convert exp to milliseconds before comparing
if (Date.now() > decoded.exp * 1000) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
}

// OR convert Date.now() to seconds
if (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > decoded.exp) {
  return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
}
```

---

## Complete Hardened Middleware

```javascript
const jwt = require("jsonwebtoken");

function authenticateToken(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers["authorization"];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(" ")[1]; // Bearer <token>

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "No token provided" });
  }

  let decoded;
  try {
    // Option A: Let jsonwebtoken handle expiry automatically (recommended)
    decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
  } catch (err) {
    // jwt.verify throws TokenExpiredError, JsonWebTokenError, etc.
    if (err.name === "TokenExpiredError") {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
    }
    if (err.name === "JsonWebTokenError") {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid token" });
    }
    return res.status(500).json({ error: "Token verification failed" });
  }

  // Option B: Manual check if you need it (e.g., after manual decode)
  // Guard against tokens issued without an exp field
  if (decoded.exp === undefined) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token has no expiry" });
  }

  const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (nowInSeconds > decoded.exp) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Token expired" });
  }

  req.user = decoded;
  next();
}

module.exports = authenticateToken;
```

---

## Why Use `jwt.verify()` Over Manual Checking

| Approach | Handles exp | Handles signature | Handles nbf | Recommended |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `jwt.verify()` | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ Yes |
| `jwt.decode()` + manual | ⚠️ error-prone | ❌ No | ❌ No | ❌ No |

**Key point:** `jwt.decode()` does **not** verify the signature — a malicious user could forge any payload. Always use `jwt.verify()` with your secret.

---

## Other Things to Check

```javascript
// 1. Ensure your secret matches what was used to sign the token
const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET); // must match signing secret

// 2. Check your token is actually being signed with an expiry
const token = jwt.sign({ userId: 123 }, process.env.JWT_SECRET, {
  expiresIn: "1h", // ← required, or exp will be missing
});

// 3. Watch for clock skew between servers (add a small buffer)
const decoded = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, {
  clockTolerance: 30, // allow 30 seconds of clock skew
});
```

The units mismatch (`exp * 1000`) is almost certainly your bug, but switching to `jwt.verify()` handles all these edge cases automatically.
